1
00:00:02,215 --> 00:00:04,351
So let's build our first custom list

2
00:00:04,351 --> 00:00:06,679
structure, and that will be a stack.

3
00:00:07,562 --> 00:00:10,244
A stack is a special kind of array, we could

4
00:00:10,244 --> 00:00:13,118
say, actually kind of a simplified array if

5
00:00:13,118 --> 00:00:14,129
you want to call it like this.

6
00:00:15,061 --> 00:00:17,421
Now here's an example. Let's stick to the

7
00:00:17,437 --> 00:00:19,155
array example. We have an array with 2

8
00:00:19,203 --> 00:00:20,873
items, pizza and apples.

9
00:00:21,869 --> 00:00:24,679
Now if that weren't a regular array but a

10
00:00:24,679 --> 00:00:28,340
stack, then there would only be 2 operations

11
00:00:28,693 --> 00:00:31,792
that we can perform to add and remove items.

12
00:00:32,611 --> 00:00:35,935
For adding items, we can push a new item

13
00:00:36,143 --> 00:00:36,978
onto the stack

14
00:00:37,524 --> 00:00:39,901
and it will always be the topmost item then.

15
00:00:40,270 --> 00:00:43,385
So new items are always pushed to the top,

16
00:00:43,851 --> 00:00:45,055
on top of the stack.

17
00:00:45,617 --> 00:00:47,351
And in an array, that simply means it's now

18
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:49,150
at the end of the array, you could say.

19
00:00:50,194 --> 00:00:52,939
And if we then want to read an item from the

20
00:00:52,939 --> 00:00:55,987
stack, we can pop it off, which means we

21
00:00:55,987 --> 00:00:57,110
remove it from the top.

22
00:00:57,831 --> 00:01:00,333
And therefore, such a stack data structure

23
00:01:00,782 --> 00:01:01,119
is a

24
00:01:01,664 --> 00:01:05,561
LIFO data structure, last in, first out. The

25
00:01:05,673 --> 00:01:08,175
item we added last is the first item we'll

26
00:01:08,175 --> 00:01:08,464
remove.

27
00:01:09,442 --> 00:01:12,473
And unlike in a regular array, we can't add

28
00:01:12,649 --> 00:01:15,536
item in the middle or at the beginning, and

29
00:01:15,552 --> 00:01:18,118
we also can't remove items from there. We

30
00:01:18,118 --> 00:01:20,846
can only add them to the top or remove them

31
00:01:20,846 --> 00:01:23,239
from the top with pushing and popping. And

32
00:01:23,255 --> 00:01:25,439
that's why I call it a simplified array

33
00:01:25,503 --> 00:01:27,960
because there's way less we can do with it.

34
00:01:28,780 --> 00:01:28,892
Now,

35
00:01:29,438 --> 00:01:32,490
why and when might we need such a stack?

36
00:01:32,971 --> 00:01:34,706
There are a couple of examples.

37
00:01:35,605 --> 00:01:38,496
One example can be shown in JavaScript and

38
00:01:38,577 --> 00:01:39,862
in the browser itself.

39
00:01:40,584 --> 00:01:41,676
Let's add a function

40
00:01:42,206 --> 00:01:43,218
printResult

41
00:01:44,616 --> 00:01:47,153
and there I want some result and I simply

42
00:01:47,185 --> 00:01:49,642
console.log that. So it's a very simple

43
00:01:49,674 --> 00:01:51,375
function, but I just wanna show you

44
00:01:51,375 --> 00:01:51,728
something.

45
00:01:53,028 --> 00:01:55,804
Then we have another function, add, and that

46
00:01:55,916 --> 00:01:57,537
takes 2 numbers, let's say,

47
00:01:58,163 --> 00:02:01,453
and it then calls printResult and passes the

48
00:02:01,453 --> 00:02:03,282
result of that addition to it

49
00:02:04,133 --> 00:02:05,288
so that we can output it here.

50
00:02:06,042 --> 00:02:08,337
And then here I call that with 2 numbers.

51
00:02:09,171 --> 00:02:10,231
Now if we save that

52
00:02:11,402 --> 00:02:13,568
and we open that in the browser, we see 6.

53
00:02:13,825 --> 00:02:16,040
Now that's probably not too surprising. I

54
00:02:16,056 --> 00:02:17,106
want to show you something else.

55
00:02:17,848 --> 00:02:20,706
In the Chrome DevTools, go to Sources here,

56
00:02:22,288 --> 00:02:23,934
and there you should find your source file

57
00:02:24,144 --> 00:02:25,307
with the code we just wrote.

58
00:02:26,083 --> 00:02:28,508
Now in there, let's add a breakpoint inside

59
00:02:28,508 --> 00:02:28,638
of

60
00:02:29,236 --> 00:02:31,645
printResult by simply clicking here on the

61
00:02:31,661 --> 00:02:32,486
line next to it

62
00:02:33,181 --> 00:02:34,718
and then reload the page. And now code

63
00:02:34,718 --> 00:02:35,982
execution will pause here.

64
00:02:36,776 --> 00:02:39,190
Now I don't wanna debug that code, it's not

65
00:02:39,190 --> 00:02:39,854
that difficult.

66
00:02:40,405 --> 00:02:42,139
I wanna draw your attention to that thing

67
00:02:42,253 --> 00:02:43,955
here in the bottom left corner.

68
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:46,954
This is the so-called call stack in

69
00:02:47,067 --> 00:02:49,730
JavaScript. And yes, you heard that right,

70
00:02:49,874 --> 00:02:51,318
it's a call stack.

71
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:56,002
JavaScript uses such a stack approach, such

72
00:02:56,002 --> 00:02:57,478
a stack structure

73
00:02:58,055 --> 00:02:59,066
for managing your

74
00:02:59,659 --> 00:03:02,034
code execution flow, you could say.

75
00:03:02,804 --> 00:03:05,370
In JavaScript, only one function can run at

76
00:03:05,370 --> 00:03:07,504
the same time, but of course, as we're doing

77
00:03:07,536 --> 00:03:10,423
it here, functions can call each other. So

78
00:03:10,647 --> 00:03:12,970
the add function calls printResult, and

79
00:03:12,970 --> 00:03:15,759
therefore the add function will only fully

80
00:03:15,791 --> 00:03:17,842
be done when printResult is done.

81
00:03:18,419 --> 00:03:21,447
Therefore, add kind of needs to wait until

82
00:03:21,559 --> 00:03:22,569
printResult is done.

83
00:03:23,162 --> 00:03:25,373
And again, since JavaScript doesn't run 2

84
00:03:25,469 --> 00:03:27,649
functions at the same time, it simply keeps

85
00:03:27,649 --> 00:03:30,100
track of all the functions it started

86
00:03:30,757 --> 00:03:32,104
and of the function that's currently

87
00:03:32,136 --> 00:03:34,155
running. And it does that with a stack

88
00:03:34,155 --> 00:03:35,709
structure because

89
00:03:36,302 --> 00:03:38,197
We start at the bottom. Anonymous means

90
00:03:38,229 --> 00:03:40,494
we're starting in our main script. We then

91
00:03:40,558 --> 00:03:43,609
call one function, add, so that function

92
00:03:43,609 --> 00:03:44,460
starts executing.

93
00:03:45,054 --> 00:03:46,708
And then we move on to print result.

94
00:03:47,527 --> 00:03:50,081
And it makes sense to build this up in such

95
00:03:50,129 --> 00:03:53,083
a stack structure because the last function

96
00:03:53,083 --> 00:03:55,091
that was called is always the function

97
00:03:55,091 --> 00:03:57,130
that's currently running. So that's this

98
00:03:57,403 --> 00:04:00,615
LIFO approach, last in, first out. The last

99
00:04:00,631 --> 00:04:02,205
function that was called is the first

100
00:04:02,237 --> 00:04:03,457
function that needs to complete.

101
00:04:04,163 --> 00:04:06,138
Only when that last function completed,

102
00:04:06,491 --> 00:04:08,770
printResult in this case, the function that

103
00:04:08,819 --> 00:04:10,986
was executed before that is able to

104
00:04:10,986 --> 00:04:11,323
complete.

105
00:04:12,109 --> 00:04:14,100
And that's why this is managed as a stack.

106
00:04:14,581 --> 00:04:15,978
And of course, that's a very technical

107
00:04:15,978 --> 00:04:18,289
example, but it is an example which you're

108
00:04:18,289 --> 00:04:20,167
implicitly using every day.

